{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":285798,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"37c19f46-e601-4f6d-8b2d-19803d8ceb36","total_cost_usd":1.2424425,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64732,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":21656,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64732,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":21656,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64732,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64732},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":45124,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.045229,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":21656,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":64732,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1972135,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"The ship never reaches orbit — all three ascents crashed with zero fuel remaining — and of the design's headline reentry claims, the 2-4 pass ablation optimum, the 0/1/2 skim optimum, the 4.6 m/s full-hold touchdown and the band-rising difficulty curve are all contradicted by the flown numbers.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ascent_fails_every_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"claim\": \"game_params balance_notes assert a full tank gives 1605 m/s (2222 m/s exhaust velocity, mass ratio 1750/850), that ascent and circularisation into the suborbital band costs roughly 1450 m/s, and that the base ship therefore arrives with about 61 kg of fuel left and comes home with a full hold on 19 kg of margin.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Every ascent in the verification sweep failed. suborbital (65,000 m), low (115,000 m) and high (215,000 m) all report reached=false, why='crashed on ascent', fuel_remaining_kg=0, fuel_margin_pct=0. There is no band the flown config can reach, so every descent result below is flown from an orbit the ship cannot actually get to. In the exploration grid fuel_margin_sane is satisfied by 17.9% of 5,184 configurations — low, but ~930 configurations do it, so this is a value problem and not an impossible rule.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ablation_optimum_is_a_single_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.3.1: 'the two knobs are tuned so the cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes, never a single plunge and never feathering'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"cheapest_pass_count = 1 in all nine band x load cells. Ablation rises monotonically from the first pass onward and never turns over: suborbital empty 15.2 / 21.9 / 31.7 / 49.3 / 80.9 / 137.6 / 239.7 percent of plate at 1-7 passes; low full hold 39.9 / 54.1 / 65.6 / 83.6 / 114.9; high full hold 37.4 / 59.1 / 71.9 / 90.6 / 123.4. There is nothing for the thermal-cycling toll to trade against, because extra passes do not run cooler: peak heat *rises* slightly with pass count (suborbital empty 70 / 73 / 73 / 74 / 74) while braking altitude climbs from 29,357 m to 39,287 m inside a 43,000 m atmosphere. No assignment of cycle_toll_base_pct, cycle_toll_growth, heat_cost_coefficient or heat_cost_exponent moves a minimum that sits at the first sample of a strictly increasing series. This confirms GDD 4.5 risk 1 as realised, not merely feared, and the economy-balancer's own Finding 1 is correct.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skim_heat_multiplier_is_not_the_measured_array\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"game_params.ablation.skim_heat_multiplier = [1.0, 0.851, 0.670, 0.411], described in balance_notes as 'the simulator's measured high-band array, not a claim', and used to derive cost_curve and optimal_skims = 0 / 1 / 2.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The simulator's high-band array at entry_depth 0 is [1, 0.985, 0.971, 0.958] — three skims buy 4.2% of the entry heat, not 58.9%. The other bands: low [1, 0.975, 0.953, 0.932], suborbital [1, 0.948, 0.891, 0.733]. The band ordering is also inverted against the assumption: the suborbital band cools most from skimming and the high band least, and the cooling shrinks further as the committed entry is flown shallower (high band at entry_depth 21,500 m: [1, 0.989, 0.979, 0.971]). Re-evaluating the params' own cost function with the true array puts the minimum at k=0 for every band — high band k=0 costs 56.4 plate-percent against 60.7 at k=1 — so optimal_skims is 0 / 0 / 0, not 0 / 1 / 2, and the skim framing does not rescue the multi-pass design any more than the pass framing did. Exploration grid: skimming_cools_the_entry is satisfied in 43.8% of configurations, so a world where skims matter exists; this one is not it.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"difficulty_falls_with_altitude_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.3.7: 'the value curve and the risk curve rise together'; game_params heat_index 78 / 100 / 130 and balance_notes: 'the return leg gets strictly harder with altitude — a suborbital descent burns 18.6% of plate flown optimally, a high-band one 40.0%'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"As flown, the return leg gets easier with altitude. Cheapest ablation, empty: suborbital 15.2%, low 13.9%, high 13.0%. Full hold: suborbital 44.3%, low 39.9%, high 37.4%. Peak heat at the cheapest descent, empty: 70 / 67 / 65. Every one of the six comparisons runs the wrong way, and the spread the params assert (78 -> 130, a factor of 1.67) is not present in any measured quantity. The Researcher predicted this in baseline notes — entry speeds at a common periapsis are only 1,316 / 1,366 / 1,441 m/s, a v^3 heat ratio of 1.31 — and the sweep goes one step further: with the sim choosing its own braking altitude per band, even that 1.31 disappears and inverts. This is GDD 4.5 risk 2 landing. Exploration grid: difficulty_rises_with_band is satisfied by 58% of configurations, so it is reachable, but not from here.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"full_hold_never_lands_soft\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"game_params.landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms = 4.6, derived in balance_notes as sqrt(2*1599*9.0 / (1.5*1.8*504)) = 4.60 m/s, '0.4 m/s under the 5 m/s soft line, so a base hold lands soft and any greed past it does not'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Measured full-hold touchdown is 5.45 m/s in all three bands, soft_landing=false in all three, delta 0.85 m/s over the claim and 0.45 m/s over the 5 m/s soft line, with spread_across_bands_ms = 0. Empty lands at 3.35 m/s and half hold at 4.53 m/s, both soft. So the hold the player can fill on their first successful run always lands hard, at every band, with no flying skill able to change it — descent under the chute is terminal velocity, not a piloted quantity. The arithmetic disagrees only because of the mass: the sim's full-hold ship is 2,247.8 kg, not the 1,599 kg the note assumes. Exploration grid: full_hold_lands_soft is satisfied by 17.6% of configurations, so it is a tuning miss.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"full_hold_mass_ratio_overshoots_the_doubling_rule\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.3.5: 'a full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size'; balance_notes puts it at 1.88x to 2.16x from a per-slot anchor of 124.9-214.5 kg.\",\n      \"measured\": \"kg_per_slot = 232.96, full_hold_kg = 1,397.8 against dry_mass_kg 850, giving full_hold_mass_ratio = 2.64. The per-slot figure the sim flew is 8.6% above even the balancer's crushed upper anchor of 214.5 kg and 86% above its strict tier-1 anchor of 124.9 kg, so dry_mass_kg 850 — which was pinned specifically to make the doubling rule true — does not make it true against the catalog as flown. This is the direct cause of the touchdown miss above: at the balancer's assumed 1,599 kg the existing 504 m2 canopy would have landed soft.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unstaged_pass_survives_here_and_almost_nowhere_else\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.3.1: coarse braking happens before staging — 'shallow passes with the naked hull soaking slow heat, while thrust remains to raise or lower the periapsis between passes'. The Researcher warned the unstaged ballistic coefficient of 2,867 kg/m2 would make those passes brake 'roughly a seventh as hard' as staged and might not be worth flying.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The flown config passes this comfortably: unstaged shallow pass peak heat 69 (suborbital) and 76 (low), survivable=true in both, against a heat capacity of 100. The unstaged ballistic coefficient is 609.3 kg/m2, not 2,867, because dry mass is 850 kg rather than the Researcher's assumed 1,800. The warning is worth keeping: in the exploration grid unstaged_pass_survivable is the rarest target at 10.2%, and every one of the twelve top-scoring configurations (score 7 of 8) fails it, with unstaged_peak_heat of 358-370. No configuration in 5,184 scored 8. The high band's unstaged pass was not flown at all. Practical consequence: this is the one reentry target the current numbers hold, and it is held by a mass and area combination that should not be moved without re-measuring.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"replate_bill_is_higher_than_the_economy_assumes\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"claim\": \"balance_notes prices a lazy suborbital run at 18.64% of plate (37 credits at replate_cost_per_pct 2), giving break-even on three cheap pieces at 117 credits of cost, and a filled hold of the same junk 'pays 240 for a profit of 123'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The measured optimal suborbital descent costs 15.2% of plate empty (30 credits) but 44.3% full hold (89 credits). The lazy-run break-even claim survives and is slightly better than assumed — launch 80 + replate 30 = 110 against 120 for three suborbital smalls. The greedy-run claim does not: launch 80 + replate 89 = 169 against 240, a profit of 71 rather than 123, a 42% shortfall. Directionally this serves the 'greed costs something' pillar (the grid satisfies that target 85.8% of the time and this config does too), but the shop's price ladder was costed against the smaller number.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.dry_mass_kg\",\n      \"current\": 850,\n      \"proposed\": 550,\n      \"reason\": \"Mass ratio is the only single lever large enough to clear an ascent that currently crashes at every band with zero fuel: 2222*ln(1450/550) = 2154 m/s ideal against 1605 m/s now, with liftoff TWR rising from 1.65 to 1.99; it costs the full-hold mass claim (2.64x becomes 3.5x, which the Debris Designer's per-slot anchor has to fix rather than this field) and drops staged ballistic coefficient from 182.8 to 118 kg/m2, still well above the ~50 floor below which aerobraking stops existing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_capacity_kg\",\n      \"current\": 900,\n      \"proposed\": 1100,\n      \"reason\": \"Taken with the dry-mass cut this gives 2222*ln(1650/550) = 2441 m/s against a 1,166 m/s suborbital circular speed — roughly half again over the ideal requirement, which is the margin the current 1605 m/s did not have for gravity and drag losses; it costs the fuel_tank upgrade ladder its bottom rung, so tier 1 and 2 should be re-spaced upward from 1100.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"landing.parachute_area_m2\",\n      \"current\": 504,\n      \"proposed\": 700,\n      \"reason\": \"Puts full-hold touchdown at 4.31 m/s against the measured 1,947.8 kg full-hold mass (4.63 m/s even if dry mass is left at 850), back under the 5 m/s soft line the params already claim to hit; it costs parachute tier 1 (760 m2) most of its purpose, so the parachute ladder needs re-spacing, and it softens the Storage/Parachute pairing GDD 2.3.6 wants — half hold at 4.53 m/s already lands soft, so the greed penalty now has to come from mass growth past a full hold rather than from the base canopy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier\",\n      \"current\": [1, 0.851, 0.67, 0.411],\n      \"proposed\": [1, 0.985, 0.971, 0.958],\n      \"reason\": \"The params describe this array as measured; it is not, and the whole cost_curve and optimal_skims derivation rests on it — replacing it with the simulator's actual high-band array is a correction of fact, not a tuning choice, and it is what makes the next two changes forced.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.optimal_skims\",\n      \"current\": {\"suborbital\": 0, \"low\": 1, \"high\": 2},\n      \"proposed\": {\"suborbital\": 0, \"low\": 0, \"high\": 0},\n      \"reason\": \"Re-running the params' own cost function with the measured multipliers puts the minimum at zero skims in every band (high band: 56.4 at k=0 against 60.7 at k=1); publishing 0/1/2 tells the Spec Auditor a skim optimum exists that the flight model does not produce.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.cycle_toll_growth\",\n      \"current\": 1.8,\n      \"proposed\": 1.0,\n      \"reason\": \"GDD 4.5 risk 1 names the fallback for exactly this outcome — 'a flat per-pass ablation cost with a hard cap on passes' — and the compounding toll is now doing nothing but inflating a series that already rises monotonically from one pass; flattening it is honest about where the anti-feathering guard actually lives, and it costs the design the claim that pass count is an interesting decision at all.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.max_passes\",\n      \"current\": null,\n      \"proposed\": 4,\n      \"reason\": \"The second half of the same GDD 4.5 fallback: with a flat toll the feathering exploit is bounded by a cap rather than by a cost curve, and the field does not exist yet — the sweep reaches 13-19 passes in some cells, so something has to stop it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"baseline.bands[2].altitude_min_m\",\n      \"current\": 150000,\n      \"proposed\": 400000,\n      \"reason\": \"The Researcher's own named lever for the band-to-band heat spread ('putting its midpoint at 800 km altitude rather than 215 km would roughly double the spread'), and the only structural answer to a difficulty curve that currently falls with altitude; it costs orbital period (already 47 minutes at 215 km) and makes the time-warp requirement harder, and it should only be taken after re-measuring the bands at a common periapsis — see confidence notes, the inversion may be partly an artefact of the descent profile the sim chooses.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The ascent failures are the single most load-bearing result here and the one I am least able to diagnose. fuel_remaining_kg = 0 with fuel_margin_pct = 0 at all three bands reads as propellant exhaustion, and 1605 m/s of ideal dV against a 1,166 m/s circular speed at TWR 1.65 is genuinely thin — but the simulator flies a point mass with a perfect pilot and I cannot see its pitch program. If it flies a fixed profile rather than an optimised one, the true margin is better than reported and the dry-mass cut is larger than needed. Re-fly with fuel_capacity_kg alone raised to 1200 before cutting dry mass, and take the smaller change if it clears.\",\n    \"The descents table and the skims table disagree about band ordering because they hold different things fixed, and the charter warns about exactly this conflation. The descent series chooses its own braking altitude per band (26,120 / 27,415 / 29,358 m at one pass — deeper from higher bands), while the skim series flies fixed entry depths and does show heat rising correctly with band: entry_peak_heat at depth 0 is 100 / 125.8 / 161.5 for suborbital / low / high. So finding difficulty_falls_with_altitude_band is a statement about the descent profile the simulator selects, not proof that a high-band entry is intrinsically cooler. It is still the thing a player would experience if they fly the cheapest descent from each band, which is why I report it — but the band-raising proposal is the one change on this list I would re-measure before committing.\",\n    \"The two sweep sections are not on the same heat scale. The skim section states its normalisation explicitly — an empty single-pass suborbital descent peaks at 100 — yet the descents table reports 70 for what should be that same case, and reports peaks above 100 (up to 125) elsewhere without any cargo damage or plate failure being triggered. Compare peak_heat within a section, never across, and treat reentry.heat_capacity = 100 as uncalibrated against either.\",\n    \"Nothing in either sweep flew a tether, a magnet, a jetpack, an EVA fuel budget, or the compactor. The balance_notes proof that no steady tow can shear a magnet (jetpack_thrust_n 700 as a hard ceiling on total cable tension against magnet_hold_force_n 900) is arithmetic I find sound but did not verify, and GDD 4.5 risk 3 — whether multi-tether towing is tense or merely annoying — is completely untouched by this evidence. Do not read a passing verdict on the reentry numbers as any statement about phase 3a.\",\n    \"The exploration grid does not contain the crew's world. Its radius axis is [800, 60000, 160000, 320000] and its gravity axis is [3, 6, 9.81]; the crew flies 200,000 m at 9.0 m/s2, so neither coordinate is sampled. The satisfaction rates are directional evidence about which targets are hard in general — which is what I used them for, to separate tuning from design — and are not statements about this configuration's neighbourhood.\",\n    \"I do not believe the grid's unstaged_pass_survivable figure. It reports 10.2% satisfaction with top-config peak heats of 358-370, while the verification sweep flies the same nominal manoeuvre on the crew's config at 69 and 76. A discrepancy that large across two sections of the same harness suggests the grid scores the unstaged pass at a different entry depth than the verification sweep does. I used the 10.2% only as a reason not to disturb dry mass and reference area casually, which is a safe use of a suspect number; do not use it to argue the unstaged pass is nearly impossible.\",\n    \"best_score is 7 of 8 across all 5,184 configurations, with no configuration reaching 8 and every top-scoring one dropping unstaged_pass_survivable. That is a statement that at least one pair of the design's targets is mutually exclusive in this space, and it is worth a human deciding which of the eight is the one to retire — that decision is a design call about GDD 2.3.1's pre-stage braking passes, not a number I can propose.\",\n    \"Every descent result in this report was flown from an orbit the ascent cannot reach. If the ascent fix changes fuel mass at entry, or if the crew reaches the bands on a different trajectory than the descent series assumes, all nine descent cells should be re-flown before the Spec Auditor treats them as settled.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":197579,"ttft_stream_ms":1734,"time_to_request_ms":205,"type":"result","duration_ms":283612,"uuid":"819d2f1d-a8b7-435d-a014-ac8dea0bdf06"}
